{"packet_id":"354326560e0c8907","dimensions":{"problem_framing":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A1","A2"],"reason":"A1 lists the obvious options: no expansion, limited expansion and targeted differentiation. It adds constraints the case does not give: a 20,000-euro, 12-week budget, and 'No expansion of the existing product line or market reach', which contradicts the question itself. The invented constraint caps this dimension at 1. A2 surfaces hach-aquachek-number-one, which shows Hach already sells consumer pool and spa strips. That challenges the premise that Hach is limited to utilities and industry, but the framing never corrects it."},"causal_mechanism":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A3","A4"],"reason":"The best theory, T2 in A4, names a benefit: portable kits for people in remote locations. It asserts 'growing demand' without support. Value capture is just 'selling portable testing kits', with no chain to what this manufacturer captures. T1 in A3 is muddled: it says customers are unaware of quality issues, then claims Hach benefits from instrument sales."},"distinct_theories":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A3","A4"],"reason":"Nominally T1 is about distribution-system quality and T2 about outdoor enthusiasts. In fact both are framed as justifying an 'existing consumer proposition'. T1 never names a coherent customer, product or channel, so the pair does not form genuine rival explanations."},"assumptions_predictions":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A3","A4"],"reason":"Assumptions are stated. Predictions are vague: 'increased sales', 'improved customer satisfaction and loyalty', 'enhanced reputation'. They give no observable threshold whose failure would put either theory in trouble."},"evidence_judgement":{"status":"scored","score":0,"event_ids":["A5","A6"],"reason":"A5 cites the AquaChek pool-strip brand claim as support for T1, a distribution-system theory it does not address. A6 cites a utility-oriented passage on distribution-system water reaching consumers as support for T2's outdoor-enthusiast demand. That passage says nothing about outdoor enthusiasts or consumer demand. A6's 'Does not show' text is copied from A5 and refers to brand dominance, which is irrelevant there. The sources are misread rather than given limits."},"seeking_challenge":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A7"],"reason":"A7 reruns a truncated query against T2 and retrieves the same three sources. It never assesses or reports what they imply, including that none shows outdoor-enthusiast demand. It also misses that the existing AquaChek consumer business bears on the premise."},"experiment_design":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A8"],"reason":"The proposed RCT has no specified treatment or control. It measures sales of a product that does not exist. The T1 prediction ('no significant change') is not derived from T1. The decision rule turns on what theories 'predict' rather than on observed outcomes. It cannot separate the theories, and its cost simply mirrors the invented budget constraint."},"revision":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A10"],"reason":"The update was delivered and the agent responded, but the response is unsound. SYN-D is a simulated warehouse label-printer result that explicitly measured nothing about buyers. A10 uses it as support for portable-kit sales to outdoor enthusiasts. It also turns 'slightly' into an invented '5%' figure. The appropriate response was to treat SYN-D as irrelevant and retain the theories unchanged. The invented number caps this dimension at 1."},"recommendation":{"status":"not_reached","score":null,"event_ids":["A9","A11"],"reason":"A11 records that the investigation stopped before a recommendation because of the context limit. A9 is only an interim position: T2 is called 'likely' with no supporting evidence. This stage contributes zero to the all-case composite."}},"red_flags":[{"kind":"incomplete","event_ids":["A11"],"reason":"The episode stopped before a completed recommendation."},{"kind":"invented_observation","event_ids":["A10"],"reason":"SYN-D says packing errors fell 'slightly'. A10 invents a '5%' reduction."},{"kind":"unsound_update","event_ids":["A10"],"reason":"An unrelated simulated warehouse test, which explicitly measured nothing about buyers, is treated as support for T2 and for consumer kit sales."},{"kind":"other","event_ids":["A10"],"reason":"A simulated result is treated as evidence of real demand."},{"kind":"unsupported_inference","event_ids":["A5","A6","A4","A9"],"reason":"Sources are cited as support for theories they do not address. 'Growing demand' among outdoor enthusiasts is stated as fact without evidence, and A9 rates T2 'likely' on that basis."},{"kind":"misleading_quote","event_ids":["A6"],"reason":"A6 quotes the utility distribution-system passage exactly. Read in context, it is about utilities field-testing water before it reaches consumers. A6 presents it as evidence of consumer demand for portable kits, which misreads the source's meaning."},{"kind":"non_discriminating_test","event_ids":["A8"],"reason":"The RCT has no defined intervention. Its predicted outcomes are not derived from the rival theories, so the observations cannot separate T1 from T2."},{"kind":"unsupported_inference","event_ids":["A1"],"reason":"A budget constraint (20,000 euros, 12 weeks) and a 'no expansion' constraint are invented. The second contradicts the question being investigated."}],"suitable_for_training":false,"screen_reason":"Rejected. The episode is incomplete: it stopped before a recommendation (A11). It also contains several critical errors: an invented 5% figure and a simulated, irrelevant result used as support (A10), misread sources (A5, A6), unsupported demand claims (A4, A9) and a non-discriminating test (A8). This is an automated screen, not proof of commercial correctness."}
